<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</h1><p class="st-meta">1953 CE – 1963 CE · capital Salisbury</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A British-created union of Southern Rhodesia, Northern Rhodesia and Nyasaland, rejected by African nationalists and dissolved after ten years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>White settler leaders, chiefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-huggins">Godfrey Huggins</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-rhodesia">Southern Rhodesia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roy-welensky">Roy Welensky</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a>, pressed for a union of the three British territories in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Despite African opposition, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> created the Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland, or Central African Federation, in 1953, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harare">Salisbury</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A federal government handled defence, economy and railways, while the territories kept their own governments. The federation promised racial 'partnership', but voting rights remained mostly white. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a> revenues financed growth and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kariba-dam">Kariba Dam</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a>, mainly benefiting <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-rhodesia">Southern Rhodesia</a>. The 1959 emergency in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nyasaland">Nyasaland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devlin-report">Devlin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monckton-commission">Monckton</a> reports undermined its legitimacy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> allowed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nyasaland">Nyasaland</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a> to leave, and the federation was dissolved on 31 December 1963. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> became independent in 1964, while <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-rhodesia">Southern Rhodesia</a> moved towards unilateral independence.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland.jpg" alt="Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: UnknownUnknown , vector version by Lupin, Greentubing, Fenn-O-maniC and NikNaks · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Rhodesia_and_Nyasaland_(1953%E2%80%931963).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1953 CE – 1963 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Salisbury</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Godfrey Huggins, Roy Welensky</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Shona, Chichewa, Chibemba</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Zimbabwe, Zambia, Malawi</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Central African Federation</dd></dl></aside></div>

